paths tools/f/fleet_claim.sh tools/fixtures/f/fleet_claim_control.sh .claude/rules/remember-git-nib.md tools/r/remember_git_nib.rish tools/f/fleet_baton.txt
what The claim writer makes TWO commits a lap -- the lead-in open and the close -- and carries the operator card on neither, so construction/ITINERARY.md's Git nib names HEAD~2 and remember_git_nib reds for the whole build window on every lap that claims. Measured before claiming rather than reasoned: over 204 claim-opening commits in this tree's history, 185 leave the card STALE under the guard's own predicate copied verbatim, and 184 of those are commits whose ONLY file is the board; over the last 55 openings the reading is 55 of 55 stale, standing a mean of 12.7 minutes and a maximum of 62 before a later commit repins, across a mean of 1.49 commits. That window is exactly where the ORDER clause puts the cold endurance run, so the fleet's most expensive reading is guaranteed to carry a manufactured red. Two ships met it today and neither closed it: copal wrote it as ROW TWO (20260917.061322) in a session log and handed it to incense, and it stands on NO ledger -- incense's live claim is about hash claims in commit bodies, a different subject. Rule 5 of remember-git-nib covers a commit landing AFTER the work commit and its arithmetic is already exactly right for one landing BEFORE it: the nib is HEAD read before the commit is made, since that HEAD becomes its parent. So this is a widening rather than a new shape. The cure puts it in the tool rather than in a habit: --open and --close carry the nib forward through the existing tools/fixtures/r/remember_git_nib_write.sh, ONLY when the board actually changed, so the idempotent no-op open stays byte-identical on BOTH files -- which is the leg that makes the repair provable. The writer still never commits and never pushes; it edits a second file and prints the stage line for both. One ledger row, the rule page's rule 5, a lead-in peer shape word under alias-sameness, control legs from both sides, and the baton's printed command. Touches no module source, no ceiling any peer watches, and no peer-claimed path.
